# ContextFlow — rules for AI coding assistants

ContextFlow is a Safari Web Extension (iPhone, iPad, Mac) with a SwiftUI container app and a Netlify backend. Read README.md first.

- Extension: TypeScript + Preact, bundled by esbuild (`scripts/build-extension.mjs`). Manifest V3. No Plasmo, no React, no Tailwind.
  - All UI lives in a shadow root (`src/content`). Use px units only, no global CSS.
  - All network calls go through the background worker (`src/background`). Content scripts never call APIs directly.
  - User-facing strings: `src/locales/<lang>.json` (keys must exist in all languages) → `t("key")`.
  - After changing anything in `src/`, run `pnpm run build` and commit `ContextFlow/Shared (Extension)/Resources` (CI checks it).
- Native app: `ContextFlow/` (Xcode). Purchases only in the app (StoreKit 2). The app shares the signed transaction with the extension via the App Group (`SharedDefaults.swift`, two identical copies).
- Backend: `netlify/functions/*.mts`, shared code in `netlify/shared`. Data lives in the `contextflow` schema of a Supabase project shared with another product — never touch other schemas/tables; add migrations to `supabase/migrations`.
- No accounts. Users are identified by a random install id; paid access = Apple-signed JWS verified server-side (`netlify/shared/apple.mts`).
- Apple review: purchases must stay in-app (no external payment links), keep restore/manage/terms/privacy links on the paywall, ask for consent before sending page content to AI.
- Tests: `pnpm test` (backend), `node tools/preview/e2e.mjs` (extension in Chromium against the local preview server).
